wcristalle@hotmail.com (Artemis the Ranger) writes:

> 1) Packrat Conduct
> 
> As a packrat, you are a character who just can't bear to throw
> anything away that's of any value at all. The consequence is that you
> have to clean out the *entire dungeon* and take every object ever
> created with you when you ascend -- either carrying it in your
> inventory, wearing it, or having consumed or destroyed it (i.e. by
> burning, eating, etc.)
> Exceptions to this rule would include: rocks (there's just too many of
> them), iron chains, and other (if any) objects with no monetary value;
> lodestones; gold coins (this is covered by my second suggested
> conduct); and, finally, objects that belong to shopkeepers. This means
> that you can sell objects in return for gold, but can't go on dropping
> objects in return for "credit" once the shopkeeper's store of money
> has been exhausted (otherwise, you could dump all unwanted objects
> inside a shop.) (In other words, if you have any credit when you
> ascend, you do not satisfy packrat conduct, though temporary credit
> could be allowable.)

With these rules, you could still use a shop as a dumping ground.  If
you buy something and then sell it back, you have effectively given
the shopkeeper money.  So to deposit all your junk in a shop, you
would first sell stuff until the shopkeeper runs out of money.  Then
you would repeatedly buy and resell items until he has his money back
again.  Then sell more junk, and repeat until you've dumped everything
you want.  Perhaps to stop this loophole, you should forbid buying
back anything that you've sold.

Is deliberately destroying an item really acceptable conduct for a
packrat?  If you're not willing to throw that plate mail away, I don't
think you'd be willing to destroy it with a scroll of destroy armour
either.  Feeding it to a metal-eating pet (or yourself while
polymorphed) should be acceptable, however, since that's making use of
it.  Polypiling is a gray area - you could see it as a valid attempt
to convert your junk into something better, or a banned attempt to
destroy (some of) it.
